WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.379 --> 00:00:05.430
Hey Wisecrack.

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:10.130
Jared again, and today we’re talking about
the most famous cyborg sorcerer laser-swordsman

00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:12.500
in the galaxy: Darth Vader.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.140
Perhaps no villain in the history of cinema
has been more iconic, or more important to a franchise.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.740
Is it the seductive voice of James Earl Jones?

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:22.840
"I am your father."

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:25.640
His badass sci-fi samurai armor?

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.720
His propensity for force choking haters?

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.149
Or is it something else?

00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:34.960
Perhaps Anakin Skywalker’s descent to the
dark side resonates so powerfully because

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.340
it represents something we all face - an all-consuming
fear of death.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:41.620
"I will even learn to stop people from dying."

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:46.040
Through Vader, Star Wars explores two different
philosophical responses to death.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:50.660
By exploring these competing ideologies, we
can better understand how this whiny lil’ shit

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.640
"I'm a person and my name is Anakin."

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:56.460
came to be the Dark Lord of the Sith.

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:59.820
Welcome to the Wisecrack Edition on the philosophy
of Darth Vader.

00:00:59.820 --> 00:01:03.640
What will ultimately turn Anakin Skywalker
into Darth Vader is fear—

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:07.140
"Fear of loss is a path to the dark side."

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:09.320
specifically, the fear of death.

00:01:09.329 --> 00:01:15.229
In his 1973 book The Denial of Death, cultural
anthropologist Ernest Becker argued that almost

00:01:15.229 --> 00:01:21.260
everything we do is a way of managing the
fear of our own demise and the demise
of those around us.

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.720
This anxiety, known as death denial, is especially
present in Darth Vader, and is rooted as far

00:01:26.729 --> 00:01:28.549
back as his childhood.

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:33.689
When we first meet Anakin Skywalker, he’s
an implausibly wise and articulate nine-year-old

00:01:33.689 --> 00:01:36.409
boy genius with superhuman piloting skillz.

00:01:36.409 --> 00:01:38.060
He’s the product of a virgin birth,

00:01:38.060 --> 00:01:39.040
"Who is his father?"

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:40.160
"There was no father."

00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:42.660
he’s smarter than any of the adults around him,

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:45.660
and there’s a prophecy that suggests he might just be the messiah.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.360
"The prophecy of the one who will bring balance to the force. You believe it's this boy?"

00:01:50.360 --> 00:01:54.880
Oh, by the way, Force aptitude is now
measured in midichlorians just so we can establish,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.160
from the very beginning, that Anakin is objectively
more powerful than anybody else.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.260
"His cells have the highest concentration of midichlorians I have seen in a lifeform."

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.660
But even young Jedi Jesus isn’t immune to
being scared shitless of death.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.720
"I care for you too, only I..."

00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:11.960
"Miss your mother."

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:15.440
Anakin’s distress over his mother’s mortality
will haunt him.

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.200
And as he gets older, this fear will become
a fixation.

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:19.760
"I don't sleep well anymore."

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:20.780
"Because of your mother?"

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.880
"I don't know why I keep dreaming about her."

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:26.640
Yoda figures out this fear is going to be
a problem pretty early on.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:29.520
"Your thoughts dwell on your mother."

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:31.700
"Afraid to lose her I think."

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:36.940
This could actually explain why the Jedi are
so hesitant to train Anakin on account of his age.

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:40.940
"The boy will not pass the counsel's test, master. He's too old."

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.680
Although it’s never explained in canon why
only young children are trained,

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:45.700
"He's too old."

00:02:45.760 --> 00:02:46.800
"He's too old."

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.960
the dialogue suggests that Yoda and Mace Windu are concerned about death denial and its ramifications for

00:02:51.970 --> 00:02:52.970
young Anakin.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:57.840
See, when kids are youngest, it’s easier
to indoctrinate them to not fear death.

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:02.290
Children, according to psychologist James
A. Graham, begin to understand death as permanent

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:07.900
between the ages of 5-9, but only as something
that happens to lame old people - say, your

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:08.900
mother Shmi.

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.580
It’s only later, around the age of 10, that
kids realize the permanence of death comes

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:14.970
for us all.

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:19.940
Anakin is 9 - able to understand his mother’s
mortality, and likely on the cusp of understanding

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:20.940
his own.

00:03:20.940 --> 00:03:25.200
The Jedi council ultimately allows Obi-Wan
to train Anakin anyway, but it comes at a

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.200
terrible cost.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:30.111
If Jedi aren’t trained beyond a certain
age because of their fear of death, there

00:03:30.111 --> 00:03:31.810
may be good reason.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:35.840
According to Becker, human violence is a natural
response to death denial.

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.430
As he writes: “only scapegoats can relieve
one of his own stark death fear.”

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:45.670
Doling out death gives people a sense of control
over it, whether that happens on the battlefield

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.640
or as regular ol’ homicide.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.120
For Anakin, that means a one-way ticket to
the Dark Side.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.480
Although it never made the final cut, the
original script for Episode 1 has Anakin saying

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.700
something to Padme that sums up his entire
arc: “Fear attracts the fearful.

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:03.780
He was trying to overcome his fear by squashing
you.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:04.780
Be less afraid.”

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:05.810
PADME: “And that works for you.”

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:07.020
ANAKIN: “To a point.”

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:11.430
The script foreshadows that Anakin, fearful
of losing his loved ones, will try to overcome

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:16.250
this anxiety by attempting to “squash”
death, to gain a sense of control over it,

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:17.989
rather than actually being less afraid.

00:04:17.989 --> 00:04:23.070
When Anakin finds that his mother has been
mortally wounded by Tusken Raiders, he slaughters

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.360
the entire village.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:26.860
"I killed them. I killed them all. They're dead."

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:31.160
This kind of revenge makes sense according
to Becker, who argues that killing other people

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:36.350
gives us the illusion that we can control
death, assuaging the fear that the Jedi Council

00:04:36.350 --> 00:04:37.940
warned him about.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.540
The more Anakin kills, the more death feels
like something he owns.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.540
After Anakin’s mother dies, he starts having
anxiety dreams again—this time that Padmé

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:48.770
will die from childbirth.

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:53.440
He’s ready to blame himself for both deaths;
his mother died after he left her, and he

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.660
believes his wife will die because he knocked
her up.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.400
"You die in childbirth."

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:01.300
It’s at this point that Anakin is given
a choice on how to deal with death - the Sith

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:03.680
way or the Jedi way.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.640
Anakin goes to Yoda with his concerns.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.360
"What must I do, master Yoda."

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:13.500
"Train yourself to let go of everything you fear to lose."

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:19.120
Yoda’s answer comes straight out of Buddhism—not
surprising, considering that its said Lucas specifically

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.780
based Yoda on this Tibetan Buddhist lama.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:26.440
According to the Buddhist text “The Pali
canon” , jaramarana—the desire to escape

00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:30.100
old age and death—is a dangerously seductive
human impulse.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:34.600
Nonattachment - or letting go of your desires
- is the only way to defeat it.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:39.140
And Yoda practices what he preaches: when
he meets his own death in Return of the Jedi,

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:40.900
he is so chill about it.

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:49.540
"Soon, will I rest. Yes, forever sleep. Earned it I have."

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:54.419
Becker would call Yoda a “knight of faith,”
a term he borrowed from the Danish existentialist

00:05:54.419 --> 00:05:55.900
Soren Kierkegaard.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:06:00.060
According to Becker, having real, authentic
faith in something more powerful than death

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:02.080
is a great way to manage our terror.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:04.380
For Kierkegaard, this meant faith in God.

00:06:04.380 --> 00:06:09.240
For Yoda, his nonattachment to jaramarana
is grounded in his faith in the light side

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:10.100
of the Force.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:16.900
"Death is a natural part of life. Rejoice for those around you who transform into the force."

00:06:16.940 --> 00:06:19.400
Either way, it’s not something you can choose.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:24.560
This kind of faith is, in Becker’s word,
“a matter of grace and not of human effort.”

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:28.760
Anakin doesn’t have Yoda’s faith, so he
goes to Palpatine for an answer.

00:06:28.760 --> 00:06:31.100
The Sith approach is to embrace your desires

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.580
"The Sith rely on their passion their for strength."

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:37.900
-- in this case the desire to prevent Padme’s death-aka: death denial.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.440
"You could actually... save people from death."

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:45.980
In telling him an old Sith legend about Darth
Plagueis the Wise, Palpatine lets it slip

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:49.380
that the dark side of the force may enable
you to defeat death.

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:56.320
"Ironic. He could save others from death, but not himself."

00:06:56.320 --> 00:06:58.740
"Is it possible to learn this power?"

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:00.640
"Not from a jedi."

00:07:00.740 --> 00:07:04.720
Anakin takes sides and becomes a Sith Lord—on
one condition.

00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:08.360
"Just help me save Padme's life, I cannot live without her."

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:13.800
Anakin sacrifices his name, his loyalty to
the Jedi, and even his conscience to learn

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.960
how to protect Padmé from death.

00:07:15.960 --> 00:07:22.200
In refusing to accept death, Anakin is set
down a dark path: he kills children, his wife,

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:23.810
and even tries to kill his mentor.

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:29.300
He has completely sacrificed his personality
and values and has become little more than a zombie

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.780
trapped in a machine.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:34.040
This is the terminal point of death denial,
according to Becker.

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:39.070
He writes: “the person seeks to avoid death,
but he does it by killing off so much of himself

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:43.740
and so large a spectrum of his action-world
that he is actually isolating and diminishing

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:46.310
himself and becomes as though dead.”

00:07:46.310 --> 00:07:50.889
Left with nobody to protect from death, Darth
Vader has become an instrument of power and

00:07:50.889 --> 00:07:52.010
little else.

00:07:52.010 --> 00:07:56.850
With this in mind, we can finally understand
what the hell Obi-Wan was doing in Episode IV.

00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:01.360
If The prequels were the story of how the
Jedi Order lost Anakin to Palpatine because

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:05.830
of his death denial, then it could
be argued that Kenobi’s death was the Order’s

00:08:05.830 --> 00:08:08.900
way of confronting Anakin’s ill-fated choice.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:10.990
Look back at their lightsaber duel on the
Death Star.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:14.490
It’s doesn’t look like Kenobi was
actually trying to kill Vader.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:19.090
They spar for a few minutes, and then he basically
offers himself up as a sacrifice.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:21.060
But not before saying something cryptic.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:25.680
"If you strike me down, I shall become more powerful than you could possibly imagine."

00:08:25.949 --> 00:08:29.920
Vader swings the lightsaber, and then Kenobi
is just sort of raptured.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:34.699
We could just say the first movie had a lower
special effects budget, but Vader seems confused

00:08:34.699 --> 00:08:36.240
by what just happened, too.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:41.210
Kenobi showed off the fact that he had another
solution, that made him, well, more powerful

00:08:41.210 --> 00:08:43.050
than Vader could ever imagine.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:47.380
Seeing that Kenobi was willing to embrace
death offered an alternative to Vader’s

00:08:47.380 --> 00:08:48.380
death denial.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.420
Meanwhile, the Sith still hadn’t figured
out immortality.

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:56.160
At the end of Revenge of the Sith, we find
out exactly how Kenobi learned to cheat death.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:05.540
"An old friend has learned the path to immortality. One who has returned from the netherworld of the force. Your old master."

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:06.880
"Qui-Gon."

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.860
"How to commune with him, I will teach you."

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:14.060
This doesn’t quite mean conquering death
in the traditional sense, because Kenobi and

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:16.240
Yoda are still technically dead.

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:18.500
It’s more transcending death.

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:24.140
They can interact with living Jedi by taking
the form of blue glowing Force ghost entities.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:27.740
Even Anakin gets to join the squad, after
he redeems himself.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:32.460
We can also see this discrepancy between death
denial and death acceptance in how Anakin

00:09:32.460 --> 00:09:34.140
and Luke confront death.

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:38.240
Anakin’s most telling confrontations with
death was when he indiscriminately killed

00:09:38.240 --> 00:09:40.620
the Tusken Raider’s to avenge his mother.

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:43.480
Ultimately Anakin even killed Padmé in his
rage.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:45.560
Or she just died from bad writing.

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:46.500
It isn’t really clear which.

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:47.700
"She's dying."

00:09:47.820 --> 00:09:50.180
"We don't know why, she has lost the will to live."

00:09:50.340 --> 00:09:55.260
Meanwhile, Luke is willing to give up his
own life to avoid turning in the thing he hates.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:10:00.420
"You're wrong. Soon I'll be dead. And you with me."

00:10:00.520 --> 00:10:04.260
When asked to kill Vader by Darth Sidious,
he refuses.

00:10:04.270 --> 00:10:08.830
This willingness to accept death is what truly
separates a Jedi from a Sith.

00:10:08.830 --> 00:10:13.920
It is only when Anakin takes a cue from his
son and learns to renounce his own life that

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.340
he embraces the reality of death and conquers
jaramarana.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:18.380
"But you'll die."

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:22.300
"Nothing can stop that now."

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.780
The story of Anakin Skywalker is one that
all of us have to live through.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:31.800
Jaramarana can rot out our personalities,
strip us of our values, and drive us to do

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:33.120
terrible things.

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:38.620
According to Becker, denial of death is responsible
for our drive to inflict violence on each

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:41.800
other, to conquer, and to control our surroundings.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:46.360
Evil Empires, in the real world and in the
world of Star Wars, owe their existence to

00:10:46.360 --> 00:10:48.900
our inability to accept mortality.

00:10:48.900 --> 00:10:53.300
But no matter how much of ourselves we’ve
invested in our denial, accepting the reality

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:57.700
of death can give us dignity, build our courage,
and expand our compassion.

00:10:57.700 --> 00:11:01.649
It’s never too late for Darth Vader, and
it’s never too late for us.

00:11:01.649 --> 00:11:06.140
As long as you’re still breathing, you can
come to terms with the reality of death—or

00:11:06.140 --> 00:11:08.960
deny it, and risk giving  into the Dark Side.

